  "query_preview": "..\nThursday through Sunday: A cool and unsettled pattern will\ncontinue the next few days as troughing lingers off the Pacific\ncoast. Despite deep troughing, it will start to fill in,\nlimiting the amount of precipitation that will fall. Showers\nwill be limited to the northern mountains and Cascades with\nlight snow above 2500-3000 feet. Accumulations in these areas\nwill be minor, ranging from a dusting to an inch. Deepening SW \nflow Saturday and Sunday will bring plentiful cloud cover and \nchances for rain and high mountain snow, primarily to the \neastern third of WA and north ID. \n\nThe forecast for Saturday evening looks potentially soggy,\nmostly east of Ritzville, with a 20\u201350% chance of rain. The\ngreatest probabilities will be in southeastern Washington and\nthe southern Idaho Panhandle. Evening temperatures will fall\ninto the low to mid 40s, accompanied by light northeast winds\naround 1-4 mph. The chance of seeing 0.25 inches or more of rain\nfrom 4 AM Saturday to 4 AM Monday is less than 10% west of \nRitzville, 10-20% for eastern Washington and northern Idaho \nnorth of I-90, and 20-60% for areas south of I-90. \n\nMonday through Thursday: Early next week will start off mostly\ndry, with high confidence in high pressure building into the\nregion. A weak wave moving through northern BC and Alberta will\nbring a slight chance of sprinkles near the Canadian border on\nTuesday. I am monitoring the potential for air stagnation in\nthis pattern, as models indicate shallow boundary layers with\nlight winds. By Tuesday, temperatures will climb 2 to 7 degrees\nabove normal, reaching the 50s, and will remain there through at\nleast Thursday. /DB",
